/-! # The `(2,3)` ladder at a non-vacuum argument: the `e_1` ray closes, the others do not

`HJO.Sym.qop_two_three_axis_eq_ladder` (`HJO/Shuffle/MellitSlopeLadderLhs.lean`) makes every
collinear slope operator a normalised bracket of one ladder rung against `D_1`,

  `Q_{2k,3k} = M⁻¹ ( L_{k-1} D_1 - D_1 L_{k-1} )`,  `L_j = (ad_{2,3})^j (D_2)`,  `k ≥ 1`,

with no case split, and `HJO.Sym.theta_mem_dopTwoAlgebra` puts the whole image of a `(2,3)` slope
homomorphism inside `L[D_1, D_2]`. Because `Θ` is an algebra map into **operators**, an expansion of
`CopComp q α 1` in the axis generators does not by itself deliver `Θ(CopComp q α 1)(1)`: each factor
of the resulting word is handed a non-vacuum vector. This file evaluates the ladder at non-vacuum
arguments, and says exactly how far that goes.

## The engine: `e_1` is the index shift

`HJO.Sym.dop_elemSymm_one_mul` reads `D_k(e_1f) = e_1D_kf + MD_{k+1}f`. As an
operator identity that is a statement about one commutator, and it is the only input this file has:

  `HJO.Sym.shiftStep_dop`:  `T(D_k) = -M·D_{k+1}`,   `T A := ad(e_1) A = E_1A - AE_1`,

with `HJO.DiffOrder.ad` the commutator against a multiplication operator. `HJO.DiffOrder.ad_mul`
makes `T` a derivation of the operator algebra, so `T` is a *derivation that shifts the index of
`HJO.Sym.DopInt` by one*. Everything below is that one fact plus Leibniz.

Four consequences, all of them unconditional in `q` and `u`:

* `HJO.Sym.apply_elemSymm_one_mul` — `A(e_1f) = e_1·Af - (TA)f` for **every** operator `A`. This is
  `HJO.DiffOrder.ad_apply` rearranged; the content is that `T` of a `D`-word is again a `D`-word.
* `HJO.Sym.apply_elemSymm_one_pow_mul` — the Taylor formula on the `e_1` ray,
  `A(e_1^m f) = ∑_{i≤m} (-1)^i binom(m,i)·e_1^{m-i}·(T^iA)f`. Iterating the one-step rule and
  collecting by Pascal. Note the spelling: keeping the *unnormalised* `T = ad(e_1)` rather than
  `M⁻¹ad(e_1)` makes this true with no hypothesis on `q, u` at all, the factors of `M` being carried
  by `HJO.Sym.iterate_shiftStep_dop` (`T^i(D_k) = (-M)^i·D_{k+i}`) where they are visible.
* `HJO.Sym.shiftStep_mem_dopAlgebra` — `T` maps `HJO.Sym.DopAlgebra` into itself, by induction over
  `Algebra.adjoin`. With `HJO.Sym.dopTwoAlgebra_le_dopAlgebra` this covers `L[D_1,D_2]` and hence
  the whole image of `Θ`.
* `HJO.Sym.shiftStep_ladderTwoThree` — `T` against one rung of the ladder:
  `T(ad_{2,3}A) = ad_{2,3}(TA) + M⁻¹(A·T(Q_{2,3}) - T(Q_{2,3})·A)`, so the `T`-images of the rungs
  satisfy a recursion of the same shape as the rungs themselves, driven by the single new operator
  `T(Q_{2,3}) = -(D_3D_1 - D_1D_3)` (`HJO.Sym.shiftStep_qop_two_three`).

## What that gives at `(2,3)`

`HJO.Sym.shiftStep_qop_two_three_axis` closes the bracket at every `k ≥ 1`:

  `T(Q_{2k,3k}) = M⁻¹((TL_{k-1})D_1 - D_1(TL_{k-1})) - (L_{k-1}D_2 - D_2L_{k-1})`,

an operator of `L[D_1,D_2]` again, with `TL_{k-1}` computed by the recursion above from
`TL_0 = -M·D_3` (`HJO.Sym.shiftStep_iterate_ladderTwoThree`,
`HJO.Sym.shiftStep_iterate_ladderTwoThree_zero`). So:

* `HJO.Sym.qop_two_three_axis_apply_elemSymm_one` — `Q_{2k,3k}(e_1)` in closed form at **every**
  `k ≥ 1`, as `e_1·Q_{2k,3k}(1)` corrected by two brackets read at the vacuum.
* `HJO.Sym.qop_two_three_axis_apply_elemSymm_one_pow` — `Q_{2k,3k}(e_1^m)` at every `k` and `m`, as
  the binomial sum in the vacuum values of `T^iQ_{2k,3k}`.
* `HJO.Sym.theta_apply_elemSymm_one_pow` — the same for every value of every `(2,3)` slope
  homomorphism, together with the statement that each operator the sum names is still in
  `HJO.Sym.DopAlgebra`. This is the closure the creation side wanted: on the `e_1` ray, no value of
  `Θ` ever leaves the algebra generated by the basic operators, and every such value is a
  combination of **vacuum** values of operators in that algebra.

The reduction is *not* to the orbit `Q_{2,3}^m(1)`, and it cannot be: that is exactly what
`HJO.Sym.collinearBracket_apply_one_not_determined_by_orbit` forbids. It is to the vacuum values of
a strictly larger, explicitly generated family — the words in `D_1, D_2` whose length grows with the
degree, which is the object `HJO/Shuffle/MellitSlopeLadderLhs.lean` already identified as what
the creation side actually needs.

## The pin

`HJO.Sym.qop_two_three_apply_elemSymm_one_pin` recomputes `Q_{2,3}(e_1)` by this route alone —
`HJO.Sym.apply_elemSymm_one_mul` at `f = 1` and `HJO.Sym.shiftStep_qop_two_three`, giving
`Q_{2,3}(e_1) = e_1·Q_{2,3}(1) - D_3(e_1) + D_1(e_3)` — and then evaluates that from the Pieri rule
and the vacuum values, arriving at the expression
`HJO.Sym.qop_two_three_apply_elemSymm_one` obtained in
`HJO/Shuffle/MellitQopThreeFour.lean` by grinding the commutator. The two routes share nothing
but `HJO.Sym.DopInt` itself, so the agreement of all four coefficients — including the `e_4`
coefficient, which needs `κ(e_3)` on one side and `κ(e_1)` alone on the other — pins the signs and
the index bookkeeping of the whole chain.

## What is *not* obtained, and why

**The clause at general `α` is not obtained**, and the obstruction is sharp rather than a matter of
effort. `Λ` is generated by `e_1, e_2, e_3, …`, and the powers `e_1^m` span only `L[e_1]`, a single
line of generators. Passing to a general `h`-monomial needs the shift in the `e_n` direction for
`n ≥ 2`, and there is no such shift *inside the operator algebra*:

  `HJO.Sym.ad_elemSymm_dop`:  `ad(e_n)(D_k) = -∑_{1≤s≤n} κ(e_s)·E_{n-s}D_{k+s}`

— every term carries a multiplication operator `E_{n-s}` standing to the **left** of a basic
operator, and only at `n = 1` is that factor `E_0 = 1`. So `ad(e_n)` does not map
`HJO.Sym.DopAlgebra` into itself for `n ≥ 2`, there is no Taylor formula in the `e_n` direction with
coefficients in that algebra, and the induction that produced
`HJO.Sym.apply_elemSymm_one_pow_mul` has nothing to run on. What `HJO.Sym.ad_elemSymm_dop` does
supply is the straightening rule for the *larger* algebra generated by the basic operators together
with the multiplication operators; whether that algebra is spanned by `E_g·(D\text{-word})`, which
would settle a general argument, is not proved here.

Nothing in this file touches the equation the `hlhs` clause asserts. It computes one side of it on a
ray.

## Genericity

`HJO.Sym.shiftStep_dop`, `HJO.Sym.iterate_shiftStep_dop`, `HJO.Sym.apply_elemSymm_one_mul`,
`HJO.Sym.apply_elemSymm_one_pow_mul`, `HJO.Sym.shiftStep_bracket_dop_two_one`,
`HJO.Sym.shiftStep_ladderTwoThree`, `HJO.Sym.shiftStep_iterate_ladderTwoThree`,
`HJO.Sym.shiftStep_iterate_ladderTwoThree_zero`, `HJO.Sym.ad_elemSymm_dop`,
`HJO.Sym.shiftStep_mem_dopAlgebra`, `HJO.Sym.iterate_shiftStep_mem_dopAlgebra`,
`HJO.Sym.dopTwoAlgebra_le_dopAlgebra`, `HJO.Sym.qop_two_three_axis_apply_elemSymm_one_pow`,
`HJO.Sym.qop_two_three_axis_mem_dopAlgebra`: **none**. Every `M⁻¹` that appears is `HJO.Sym.Qop`'s
own and is carried in the statement rather than cancelled, so at `M = 0` these read `0 = 0` for
`k ≥ 2` — `HJO.Sym.Qop`'s totalisation, true and empty, not a false clause. In particular
`HJO.Sym.qop_two_three_axis_apply_elemSymm_one_pow` is unconditional *and* degenerate at `M = 0`,
where `T` kills every generator and both sides vanish.

`HJO.Sym.shiftStep_qop_two_three`, `HJO.Sym.shiftStep_qop_two_three_axis`,
`HJO.Sym.qop_two_three_axis_apply_elemSymm_one`,
`HJO.Sym.qop_two_three_apply_elemSymm_one_of_shiftStep`,
`HJO.Sym.elemSymm_one_mul_qop_two_three_sub_dop_three_add_dop_one`,
`HJO.Sym.qop_two_three_apply_elemSymm_one_pin`: `M = (1-q)(1-u) ≠ 0`, and nothing else. It is used
for exactly one thing, the cancellation `M⁻¹M = 1` that turns `M⁻¹·(-M)·B` into `-B`; the hypothesis
is explicit in each statement and no letter here silently becomes the zero map. `M ≠ 0` is `q ≠ 1`
and `u ≠ 1`, and holds at algebraically independent `q, u`.

`HJO.Sym.theta_apply_elemSymm_one_pow`: `CharZero L`, `qu ≠ 0` and `(qu)^{j+1} ≠ 1` for every `j` —
verbatim `HJO.Sym.theta_mem_dopTwoAlgebra`'s, i.e. the free generation of `Λ` by the axis
generators, without which `Θf` is not determined at all. Algebraic independence of `q, u` supplies
them. The evaluation half of the conjunction needs nothing; the membership half needs all three.

## Implementation notes

These are structural facts about `HJO.Sym.DopInt`, `HJO.Sym.Qop`, `HJO.Sym.DopAlgebra` and
`HJO.Sym.IsSlopeHom`, and a negative fact about one route.

## References

The objects involved are `HJO.Sym.DopInt`, `HJO.Sym.Qop`, `HJO.Sym.DopAlgebra`,
`HJO.Sym.IsSlopeHom`, `HJO.Sym.axisGen`, `HJO.Sym.Cop`,
`HJO.Sym.plethShift`, `HJO.Sym.dop_elemSymm_one_mul`, `HJO.Sym.coprime_ladder`,
`HJO.Mellit.lhsRewrite_sweepWitness`.
